WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:01.780
- If you were alone in a room

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:03.444
with someone who's taken a
life, what would you ask?

00:00:03.444 --> 00:00:06.027
(mellow music)

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:10.363
- What's your name?

00:00:10.363 --> 00:00:11.230
- Oh, my name's Simon.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:12.410
- Hi, Simon. How are you?

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:13.243
- Nice to meet you.

00:00:13.243 --> 00:00:14.600
- It's nice to meet you, too.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.630
- What exactly happened?

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:19.493
- When I was 19, I got behind
the wheel while intoxicated.

00:00:19.493 --> 00:00:20.326
- What were you thinking?

00:00:20.326 --> 00:00:21.740
- I didn't have an opportunity to think.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:23.780
It was just an, "Oh, (beep)" moment,

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.490
and then two of my friends
passed away in my car.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:29.340
- Was it the first time you drunk drove?

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.280
- It was not. I was 19 at the time.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.500
I mean, I didn't do it every
single day or every weekend,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.310
but I think I did it
maybe five or six times.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.000
- What did you get convicted with?

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.040
- Aggravated DUI.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.200
The aggravated DUI held more time,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.710
so they dropped the reckless homicide,

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.160
just so I got more time.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.290
- Do you feel like the
DA treated you fairly?

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.830
- I just vividly remember
the DA kind of trying

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.990
to paint a picture of
you intentionally wanting

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.490
to get behind the wheel when, you know,

00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:57.280
you never would've even contemplated it

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.100
if that was a possibility that, you know,

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.530
you would've entered the car.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:02.480
- What is your relationship
like with your parents?

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.610
How did they handle what happened?

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.580
And kind of what was their reaction to it?

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:08.460
- My dad's always just been there for me.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.070
He's always been very
supportive of anything I did.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:14.870
I mean, he's forgiven me,
even if he was even mad at me.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:17.160
That was the second time I
actually heard my dad cry.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.840
The first time was when my mom passed.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.130
And the second time was over the phone

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.330
when I told him that I was at the hospital

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:24.660
and that two my friends passed away.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:25.810
But he's always been there for me,

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:28.410
and that's somebody I could always go to,

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:29.690
regardless of anything.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.520
- Are there still people in
that community that you keep

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.630
in touch with today that you're close to?

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.980
- A lot of people just
kind of wrote me off

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:37.170
after I got out of prison

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.890
'cause I spent eight and
a half years in the pen,

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.160
and all the childhood
friends that I did have,

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:43.510
they never spoke to me again.

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:46.110
And they kind of, I guess,
just had a bad taste

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:47.950
in their mouths just about what I did,

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:49.780
and I guess they didn't
wanna associate me with them,

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.310
which is perfectly fine.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:52.380
I totally understand it.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.690
The only person I spoke to was my dad.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.560
- I hope I don't sound rude
when I ask this, but like-

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.270
- Nah, just be open.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:01.820
- Do you consider yourself a killer?

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:03.860
- Do I consider myself a killer?

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.450
Society's definition of a killer,

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:07.350
I mean, is it premeditative?

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:08.800
Is it something you wanted to do?

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.970
If I could answer you,
all I could tell you

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:11.960
is I never meant to do it,

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.030
and if I could change my life to theirs,

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:16.740
I'd do it over and over again.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.430
- Understood. Sorry, again, if I-

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:19.890
- No, no, no. It's all good, man.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:21.080
- Thank you.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.240
- What would you think then
after I answer like that?

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:24.700
Am I a killer?

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.250
- No, of course not.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:27.850
- [Interviewer] What passed
most of your time in prison?

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:29.660
- Probably staring at a wall.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:32.270
There's not much you can do
unless you actually have a job

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:34.640
that requires you to go outside your cell,

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.570
which eventually I did become a cook,

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:39.050
and that was big for me
'cause it gave me routine.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:40.370
It gave me structure.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:42.940
There was a long time while I
was in there that, you know,

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:46.620
you just didn't see any
point of going further

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:50.700
because of the whole incident
and just waking up with that.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.920
- Do you see yourself ever driving again?

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.080
- That's on my bucket list.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.180
Just kind of to get my license back

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:58.570
and be able to drive, period.

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.000
Even if I don't drive any.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.140
Just having that, it
means something to me.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:06.540
I do get, sometimes, a very anxious type

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.350
of feel when I'm a passenger

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.190
and when somebody brakes
hard or something.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.310
You kind of just feel
almost a roller coaster type

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:14.880
of feeling, and it makes me
just feel a little bit anxiety.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.060
But as far as driving, I
mean, I don't know yet.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:20.490
- Have you ever dream about that accident?

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.700
- I have a reoccurring
dream that I'm in a vehicle,

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:24.900
and I can't stop it.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.890
And it's just an
anxiety-driven type of deal.

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:30.300
It's kind of like the
flashback to being in the car

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.860
and then, you know, getting
to the accident itself.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:34.130
- I'm an alcoholic.

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:37.050
I've been sober for three
years but my last...

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:38.513
I've had a couple DUIs.

00:03:39.568 --> 00:03:42.820
My last DUI I had was in an accident.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:45.290
Thank God no one was hurt, but-

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:46.810
- Unfortunately, in today's society,

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:48.390
you have to experience something

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.400
to truly grasp the effect
it could have on you.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.610
- Right, how's your mental
health been since it's happened?

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:55.577
How are you?

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:59.110
- It's hard.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:01.760
As far as thinking you
could have some type

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.430
of normality in your
life, just not a thing.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.580
I thought when I got locked
up after I'd get out,

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:08.413
just being free.

00:04:08.413 --> 00:04:12.110
That would be, I guess, the
key to finding contentness

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:13.760
with who you are.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:15.610
But I mean, there's weeks

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:17.120
that I just just don't wanna do anything.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.760
There's days where you just contemplate

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:21.420
your own life in general.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:23.650
It's a wake-up call.

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:25.100
It's a whole different
reality that you have

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.720
to set yourself to live.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.150
- What gets you up in the morning?

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:32.060
What do you do to kind of get
out of those kind of funks?

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:33.390
- The biggest things that helped me,

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.980
just realizing that you
do have a second chance

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:37.000
and people don't-

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.610
Some people didn't get a
second chance from this.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:43.810
- Have you seeked out any
sources to help you process this?

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:47.913
- I had a pretty rough time for a while,

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:50.410
and I tried therapy.

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:52.330
I don't know if it was the
wrong therapist or what,

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:54.830
but it just felt like a chore.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:56.350
It felt like just talking to somebody

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:57.720
that can't really give you an insight

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:01.650
that you haven't experienced
or thought about on your own.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.290
- I think that, myself, I would-

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:06.470
Probably my whole outlook
on life would change

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:08.550
after something like this happened to me.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:10.910
How has it affected your philosophies?

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:12.230
'Cause to me, it seems
like you've come out

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:13.820
of this a stronger person.

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:15.730
- It definitely shaped who I am,

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:18.970
and it wasn't about until,
like, three to four years in

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:22.563
before I kind of accepted my reality.

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:24.810
I met a Buddhist in prison.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.150
He was in there, ironically, for murder,

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:27.300
but he didn't kill anybody.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:30.960
I just studied a lot of different aspects

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:34.210
and perspectives that
I felt could, I guess,

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:39.210
help me find peace with
Buddhism, meditation, even yoga.

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:40.920
And I just kind of compiled that

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:45.010
into something that allowed
me to find some type of peace.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:46.260
- That's beautiful.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:49.860
I mean, for me, I had a lot
of anxiety back in college

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:54.080
and that pressure allowed me
to discover peace for myself.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:57.130
But I also sometimes feel like
if it weren't for the tragedy

00:05:57.130 --> 00:06:00.120
or the anxiety that we
have in our earlier life,

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.210
we may not ever be able to overcome that

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:03.390
and become the stronger person.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.220
So I just feel like it's beautiful to hear

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:08.320
that somebody who's had such
a tragic experience can come

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:09.810
out on the other end,

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:12.763
finding the hope and
the peace that you have.

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:16.920
That's really beautiful to hear.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:18.010
- Yeah, one thing with society

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:20.680
that I've noticed ever since I got out,

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:24.073
everybody's very oriented on
comparing themselves to others.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:26.750
It seems like anxiety is
on a whole 'nother level,

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:27.960
like you're not good enough.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:30.480
But a lot of people are
just lost in the sauce,

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:31.313
I guess you can say,

00:06:31.313 --> 00:06:34.270
and haven't been humble to
a point where they realize

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:35.840
what's really important and what's not

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:38.020
because tomorrow's not promised.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:40.690
- Have you had alcohol since the incident?

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:43.470
- I've had alcohol. I've not gotten drunk.

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:45.313
I don't like going out.
I don't like drinking.

00:06:45.313 --> 00:06:48.820
I haven't done drugs in,
I don't know, 10 years.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:50.523
I feel like staying sober is-

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.760
That's what my life is going to be

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:57.290
'cause I just, I wouldn't be happy

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:59.900
if I were to get drunk or get messed up.

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:02.900
It would just maybe bring
back a lot of anxiety

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:03.980
or a lot of pain,

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:08.150
just kind of disregarding what
you did because of drinking.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:10.820
- What's one positive things
you learned from that accident,

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:12.083
if there's any?

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:15.360
- I mean, everything has a consequence.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:17.990
So when you do something,
even if you deem it small,

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:19.510
you just gotta kind of take a step back

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:21.590
if you're about to do something stupid

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:23.940
and realize that, you
know, you could not only

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:25.430
be affecting yourself, but other people.

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:28.890
I mean, I was dumb. I
was stupid. I was young.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:30.680
You know, I feel like you
have a curtain in front

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:31.620
of your head at that age.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:33.710
I was 19. A lot of people do, rather.

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:34.840
But speaking for myself,

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:37.410
you don't think past
tomorrow or the next week.

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:39.760
You're just in the now.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:41.240
And you know, it was just

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:43.990
the whole college
partying-oriented lifestyle.

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:46.790
I mean, the person I was
back then, I was a child.

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:50.520
- It sounds you weren't even
present in your own life.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:51.580
- I feel like a lot of people are

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:53.033
in that type of predicament.

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:54.983
- Yeah.

00:07:54.983 --> 00:07:55.816
- I work in law enforcement.

00:07:55.816 --> 00:07:58.440
I'm a police officer here in LA County.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:02.630
Usually 12 to 4 o'clock is when
you get your drunk drivers.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:05.420
Certain crimes, we do
have discretion on whether

00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:07.670
or not we're going to arrest someone.

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:10.040
I don't exercise my discretion.

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:11.780
For me, it's one of those
things where it's like,

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:14.080
you're lucky that I stopped you before-

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:15.533
- Do you let them off?

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:19.600
- I'm gonna be honest with you,

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:22.020
I've probably let off one or two people.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:22.960
- I feel like it's better

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:27.960
to give people that initiation
shock of what you're doing.

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:30.710
Letting them off, I mean,
that's just, you know,

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:34.200
it's downplaying, and it's not giving them

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:35.337
that realization like,

00:08:35.337 --> 00:08:36.960
"Oh, (beep). I could just
do this and get off."

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:38.590
- No. Yeah, and I totally agree.

00:08:38.590 --> 00:08:42.070
I mean, but so everything's
case-by-case, man.

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:45.350
'Cause I mean, you get those people

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:48.670
that you genuinely feel for,

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:52.190
and they're either going
through something traumatic

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:54.190
and they made a mistake.

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:58.240
Or you have those people
that are just ignorant

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:02.222
and they think that, "Okay,
I live five minutes away.

00:09:02.222 --> 00:09:04.280
This five minute drive isn't gonna-

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:07.341
Nothing's gonna happen in
between that time," you know?

00:09:07.341 --> 00:09:08.174
- I mean, me, personally,

00:09:08.174 --> 00:09:10.606
I don't think there's
a justification, right?

00:09:10.606 --> 00:09:13.120
It's kind of just comparing
apples to oranges.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:15.733
- No, for sure. But I mean, it's-

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:19.190
- You just never know
whose life you can save by,

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:20.760
you know, giving somebody that DUI

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:22.020
and just kind of opening their mind.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:23.019
- Mm-hmm.
- Yeah.

00:09:23.019 --> 00:09:26.160
- Do you feel that the punishment
fit the crime in a sense?

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:28.930
- I've never even looked
at it in that way.

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:31.910
Just the overall incident itself,

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:33.500
it's something that never will go away.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:34.333
- [Officer] Oh, for sure.

00:09:34.333 --> 00:09:35.975
- That's kind of like...

00:09:35.975 --> 00:09:38.156
- Have you forgive yourself yet?

00:09:38.156 --> 00:09:40.463
If so, how long does it take you?

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:44.180
- I mean, that's just an
everyday type of struggle.

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:45.417
I don't really know when people ask,

00:09:45.417 --> 00:09:47.020
"Have you forgiven yourself?"

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:48.430
It could have so many meanings.

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:50.130
There's certain days
where I wake up all right.

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:52.783
There's other days where
I wake up just, you know,

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.260
feeling like you wanted to be one

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:58.881
of the passengers instead
of being the one alive.

00:09:58.881 --> 00:10:01.631
(relaxing music)

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:05.550
- I'd give you a hug right now if I could.

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:06.383
- Appreciate it.

00:10:06.383 --> 00:10:07.216
- [Cop] What up?

00:10:09.866 --> 00:10:10.699
- Nice to meet.

00:10:10.699 --> 00:10:12.416
- Yeah, good to meet you.

00:10:12.416 --> 00:10:13.249
- Hi!

00:10:13.249 --> 00:10:14.622
- Nice to meet you.

00:10:14.622 --> 00:10:15.580
- Nice to meet you.
- Nice to meet you, man.

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:18.600
- Nice to meet you.
- Nice to meet you.

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:19.433
- Can I follow your Twitter?

00:10:19.433 --> 00:10:20.266
- Absolutely, man.

00:10:20.266 --> 00:10:21.099
- Nice to meet you.

00:10:21.099 --> 00:10:21.977
- Can I give you a hug?

00:10:21.977 --> 00:10:23.310
- Sure, you can.

00:10:24.941 --> 00:10:25.774
- Thank you.

